from typing_extensions import Literal
Literal

Restricts a value to a fixed set of choices.

Example:

Literal["poem", "story", "joke"]
Why LangChain/LangGraph use this

When you do:

structured_llm = llm.with_structured_output(Route)

the model is guided to return something like:

Route(step="joke")

instead of free-form text like:

I think a joke would be best.

So the combination is:

BaseModel  -> defines structure
Literal    -> limits valid choices
Field      -> adds metadata/instructions

This is why you'll see them together constantly in LangChain, LangGraph, and agent workflows.

Common use in LLMs
class Route(BaseModel):
    step: str = Field(
        description="The next step in the routing process"
    )

The description helps the LLM understand what should be generated.

